const WAVE2_RECEIPT = [{"group": "gradle-core", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-core.md", "must_count": 18, "conflicts_resolved": ["Kafka's archive block: pub audit called it 'Gradle's own defaults, a no-op'; the task-inputs dive re-fetched upgrading_major_version_9.html and the AbstractArchiveTask javadoc — those were the PRE-9.0 defaults and kafka's wrapper pins 9.7.1. Resolved for the dive (release notes normative over audit inference): apache__kafka@940c100fab:build.gradle:362-364 is an active, total reproducibility opt-out. Makes GRADLE-CACHE-17 version-gated rather than the usual 'add the block' advice.", "Configuration cache 'on by default in Gradle 9': exemplar-build-shape.md's Contradictions section asserts it; the cc dive fetched the 9.0.0 release notes and road-to-configuration-cache and found preferred-with-automatic-fallback with the default targeted at Gradle 11. Resolved for the dive; the audit sentence is an overstatement, matching topic-map conflict 18.", "build-logic-settings severity: the build-logic dive proposed MUST; its own Contested section then records junit-team__junit-framework@35c56a8e02:settings.gradle.kts:2-4 and jjohannes's reference repo shipping the merged form, with no stated size threshold anywhere. Resolved to SHOULD (GRADLE-STRUCT-02) — Gradle's wording is comparative ('A better solution'), the cost is caching throughput not correctness, and a MUST would flag JUnit and a Gradle Developer Advocate's teaching repo.", "Must CI pass --configuration-cache? The cc dive's candidate #6 reads as yes; pub measured the flag in 3/32 workflows vs the property in 10/32. Resolved: gradle.properties is the canonical switch and the check reads it first (GRADLE-CACHE-06); a CLI flag is for overriding one job, and requiring it would flag nearly every adopter.", "validatePlugins warns or fails? java_gradle_plugin.html documents a jar-time warning; the ValidatePlugins javadoc says the task fails by default. Resolved: two distinct gates at different times — the jar-time check warns, the task fails, and a plugin author who only runs `build` never hits the second. Hence GRADLE-CACHE-16 requires the explicit invocation.", "Isolated Projects adoption (5/32) read as maturity: resolved against the 9.7.0 docs' own 'not fully enforced yet' on two of four constraint categories — adoption of the property is not adoption of a gate (GRADLE-CACHE-10), while the dead org.gradle.unsafe.* key stays a mechanical MUST (GRADLE-CACHE-09)."], "needs_another_round": ["maven-publish x configuration cache — is maven-publish fully cache-compatible on Gradle 9.7.x, partially (only the explicit-credentials path falls back per #24040), or still broadly incompatible? The cc dive fetched the issue and a March-2025 Gradle blog claim and could not resolve it; this blocks a precise GRADLE-CACHE-08 and touches GRADLE-PUB.", "GRADLE-STRUCT rows never dived — M-B-05 (two subprojects sharing a leaf name causing wrong conflict resolution with no build failure, gradle/gradle#847) and M-B-07 (a subproject gradle.properties overriding root settings the configuration cache assumes fixed): both adjudicated P0/P1, neither covered by a wave-2 dive, both silent-failure shapes.", "Remote build cache push-gating — 10 buildCache{} blocks across 6 repos but no checked-out settings file shows remote(HttpBuildCache){ isPush = ... }; 'who may push to the shared cache' is the one build-cache correctness question the corpus has not answered.", "Gradle 10 archive defaults — GRADLE-CACHE-17 is framed 9-vs-8; nothing in the 9.0.0/9.7.0 notes says whether Gradle 10 changes archive reproducibility again. Re-check when Gradle 10's release notes exist."]}, "rule_id_count": 26, "dives": [{"slug": "config-cache-contract", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-core/config-cache-contract.md", "sources_count": 15, "surprises": ["gradle/gradle#24040: Gradle 9 changed maven-publish behavior so explicit repository credentials now silently DISABLE the configuration cache for that task instead of failing the build — a build can look fully successful while quietly never being cached.", "The prior audit (exemplar-build-shape.md) itself asserts 'Gradle 9's default is already on regardless of the property,' which the primary sources (9.0.0 release notes, the road-to-configuration-cache blog) directly contradict — it's preferred-with-automatic-fallback, default still targeted at Gradle 11, not on by default in 9.x.", "org.gradle.unsafe.isolated-projects isn't just an old alias floating in blogs — the 9.7.0 release notes formally deprecate it in that release, with 0/32 exemplars using it versus 5/32 on the live org.gradle.isolated-projects key.", "Isolated Projects' own 9.7.0 docs admit two of four constraint categories (cross-build access, build-to-project access) are 'not fully enforced yet' — the feature can't yet catch everything it claims to forbid.", "gradle/gradle#18520 (build-listener ban has no full replacement API for build-start/build-finish hooks, only task-completion) is still open years after the documented fix pattern existed — the 'documented replacement' isn't actually complete for every BuildListener use case.", "The reviewer's compliance check has no dedicated task — it's just the real build/test command plus --configuration-cache, and 'clean' is the ABSENCE of a problems-summary line and HTML report, not a positive success signal from any task."]}, {"slug": "build-logic-and-catalog-seam", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-core/build-logic-and-catalog-seam.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["gradle/gradle#15383 is still fully open after nearly 6 years with a maintainer stating the gap may be architecturally unfixable (precompiled plugins are compiled before the consuming catalog is known), not merely unprioritized.", "The build-logic-settings split is not universal even among included-build adopters: junit-framework includes two ordinary modules together with no settings-only module, and jjohannes's reference repo mixes Settings and ordinary plugins in one module.", "There is no workaround at all — supported or unsupported — for using alias(libs.plugins.x) inside a precompiled script plugin's plugins{} block, because that block is evaluated against a dummy project before any catalog exists.", "dagger centralizes the string-keyed catalog workaround into a dedicated buildSrc helper file (VersionCatalogs.kt) rather than repeating it per convention plugin — a reusable pattern worth citing as the concrete fix.", "Zero corpus repos at the measured SHAs use the internal LibrariesForLibs reflection hack — the supported VersionCatalogsExtension API is universal in the checked-out convention-plugin code."]}, {"slug": "task-inputs-and-cache-hits", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-core/task-inputs-and-cache-hits.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["Kafka's block (build.gradle:362-364) is not merely 'restates defaults' as the wave-1 audit's headline framed it — direct fetch of the Gradle 9.0.0 upgrade guide shows it's a byte-for-byte copy of Gradle's own documented 3-property 'revert to pre-9 archive behaviour' snippet (including useFileSystemPermissions()), applied on a repo pinned to wrapper 9.7.1: a total, deliberate-looking opt-out of reproducibility, most plausibly an unexamined pre-9 carry-over never revisited during the Gradle 8→9 upgrade.", "Two of the three real (non-false-positive) reproducible-archive hits in the corpus (micronaut-core, spotless) are the OPPOSITE problem from kafka: they correctly set the pre-9 values for reproducibility, which is now simply dead code restating the Gradle 9 default — harmless, but a rule can't just say 'check these properties are set correctly', it has to branch on wrapper version.", "validatePlugins and the java-gradle-plugin jar-time check are two independently-implemented validations that overlap in content but differ in severity: the jar-time check only ever warns (confirmed live on the current docs page), while validatePlugins fails the build by default (confirmed via its own javadoc) — an agent that sees a warning and calls it fine can miss a check that would hard-fail.", "It could not be confirmed from any primary Gradle doc that validatePlugins is wired into check/build by default for a java-gradle-plugin module — community sources hedge with 'typically wired', so the safe normative rule is an explicit CI step, not reliance on implicit lifecycle wiring.", "The gradle/gradle docs source path for the 31 validation-problem IDs lives under a directory literally named 'unused/' (platforms/documentation/docs/src/docs/userguide/unused/validation_problems.adoc) yet the page is live, current, and matches docs.gradle.org/current/userguide/validation_problems.html exactly — the directory name is a docs-build artifact, not a deprecation signal, worth flagging so nobody discounts the source."]}]}, {"group": "gradle-plugin-dev", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-plugin-dev.md", "must_count": 15, "conflicts_resolved": ["A — Topic map conflict 1 claims `com.gradle.plugin-publish` auto-applies `com.gradleup.shadow`; the primary doc and `GradleUp__shadow@541b3be475:build.gradle.kts` (applies plugin-publish, neither shades nor relocates itself) show it only auto-publishes the shadow jar IF Shadow is applied. Resolved toward the dive: shading a plugin is opt-in and SHOULD, not the sanctioned default — the map's phrasing is corrected.", "B — testkit dive demands three MUST TestKit legs (floor/current/release-candidate); detekt, the sharpest plugin-as-product exemplar, runs two, and the RC leg exists only in gradle/actions (an Action, not a plugin). Resolved by splitting severity: floor + current MUST (GRADLE-PLUG-12), release-candidate SHOULD and scheduled-only (GRADLE-PLUG-13) — an RC break is an upstream regression and a red PR trains people to ignore the matrix.", "C — config-inventory Axis 4 left the seat for the `ocx --format json env` read open between ValueSource, BuildService and Provider. Resolved one way only on a documented impossibility ('a BuildServiceProvider cannot drive a ValueSource at configuration time'): ValueSource reads, BuildService holds the parsed value; recomputing per task is rejected for multiplying spawns and reopening the ambient-env hole.", "D — testkit dive calls `enableStricterValidation` a MUST; gates audit measures 1/32 adoption (detekt only). Resolved toward the dive with scope narrowed to modules applying `com.gradle.plugin-publish`: the mechanism decides it — stricter mode is the only check covering the plugin's EXPORTED types, whose failures land in consumers' builds. Flagged as a new commitment and as owner question Q3.", "E — `validatePlugins` presence reads as a 'this repo ships a plugin' signal; pub audit measured 10/32 files across 7 repos, 7 of them with zero Portal signal (nowinandroid, sqldelight). Resolved: route on `com.gradle.plugin-publish` (GRADLE-PLUG-20) — this is what keeps the Portal rows off the OCX SDK's own build-logic modules.", "F — M-F-07 / map conflict 5 leaves the `gradlePlugin { plugins { } }` id idiom split. Shape audit: map-key form dominant where the plugin IS the product (shadow, detekt), explicit `id = \"…\"` dominant in spring-boot. Resolved: neither is wrong — write the map-key form in new code; an idiom-only rewrite is churn, demoted from a rule to failure mode 11."], "needs_another_round": ["Settings-plugin tool provisioning — what is the right Gradle seat for `@ocx_tool`-style pinned-binary download with sha256 enforcement and a version-floor gate (custom JavaToolchainResolver, BuildService-backed download-and-verify, or ArtifactTransform), and how does each behave under the configuration cache and on a cold CI runner? config-inventory Axis 4 leaves the cell explicitly Open and 0/32 exemplars are a Settings plugin that shells to a CLI.", "Isolated Projects compatibility for a Settings plugin — M-F-04's second half is unanswered: IP reached incubating only in Gradle 9.7.0, it is not yet a declarable Compatibility-Plugin feature, and a Settings plugin registering one shared BuildService across every project is exactly the IP-hostile shape. Can such a plugin be IP-compatible on Gradle 9.7 at all, and what diagnostic proves it?", "Plugin API-surface nullability (M-F-11) — untouched by both dives. Does a plugin whose public API is consumed from a Kotlin DSL build script need JSpecify annotations, given Kotlin treats JSpecify as `strict` (errors) and JSR-305 as `warn` — i.e. does keeping JSR-305 on a plugin API break consumer build scripts under Kotlin 2.2/K2, or only warn?"]}, "rule_id_count": 21, "dives": [{"slug": "plugin-contract-and-validation", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-plugin-dev/plugin-contract-and-validation.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["validatePlugins presence is a red herring for \"does this repo publish a plugin\" — 10/32 corpus repos run it purely for internal build-logic convention modules via java-gradle-plugin, with zero Portal signal (nowinandroid, sqldelight).", "Gradle explicitly documents that a BuildServiceProvider cannot drive a ValueSource's parameters at configuration time — this fixes the ValueSource-vs-BuildService seat question as a one-way dependency, not a stylistic choice.", "The three afterEvaluate replacements aren't three interchangeable options — they map to three distinct problems (deferred reads, optional defaults, cross-plugin reaction), and the docs name exactly one narrow carve-out (fail-fast validation of your own plugin's state, last-registered-only).", "Settings plugins carry a hidden structural cost: folding one into a shared build-logic composite build forces pluginManagement{} inclusion, which reduces build-cache capability — Gradle's own fix is a separate minimal build-logic-settings build, confirmed only in gradle/gradle itself in the corpus checked.", "ExecOperations.exec accepts an explicit environment(map) that replaces rather than extends the ambient environment, giving Gradle a native primitive for OCX's site/translucent/explicit/pinned classification even though Gradle has no built-in classification scheme of its own.", "Two Gradle-10-scheduled removals are plugin-author-specific and easy to miss: Project.getProperties() and Project.container(...), both still legal in 9.x but silently breaking on the next major upgrade."]}, {"slug": "testkit-matrix-and-portal", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-plugin-dev/testkit-matrix-and-portal.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["com.gradle.plugin-publish 2.1.0 (dated to the March 2026 Gradle newsletter) auto-applies a whole separate plugin (gradle/gradle-plugin-compatibility-plugin) that makes a Configuration-Cache compatibility declaration deprecated-if-absent, with a stated intent to eventually hard-reject undeclared plugins — no cutover date announced yet.", "Shadow's own build does not shade or relocate itself despite being the shading tool, because Gradle isolates each plugins{ id(...) } plugin into its own classloader by default — the 'shade a plugin, therefore relocate everything' generalization is wrong; the mandatory-relocation case (error-prone in javac) is specifically about a shared, non-isolated classloader.", "validatePlugins running in a build tells you almost nothing about Portal publishing on its own — 10/32 exemplar files hit the task purely because java-gradle-plugin registers it for internal convention-plugin modules, with 7 of those repos showing zero other Portal signal.", "detekt runs a genuinely separate compilation (not just a different GradleRunner call) pinned to its floor Gradle version via a dedicated resolvable configuration and PluginUnderTestMetadata task, which is a stronger floor-proof than a single withGradleVersion() call.", "Shadow's functional-test target needs four explicit --add-opens JVM args to test configuration-cache behavior via GradleRunner.withDebug(), an operational wrinkle tied to a 2022 Gradle issue whose continued necessity on the current JDK 17 floor is unverified.", "Two incompatible gradlePlugin{ plugins{} } id-declaration idioms (create(\"id\"){} map-key vs explicit id=\"...\") remain in live, current use with no winner — Shadow and detekt use different but equally current syntax."]}]}, {"group": "dependencies", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-dependencies.md", "must_count": 23, "conflicts_resolved": ["enforcedPlatform + spring-boot: the topic map (M-C-07), the build-shape audit (smell 4) and BOTH Gradle dives call spring-boot a violation on a raw count of 13 hits. Re-read all 13 today: 5 are smoke-test configurations, 5 are test/intTest/systemTest, 2 are internal tooling modules, and the single `api(enforcedPlatform(...))` is at platform/spring-boot-internal-dependencies/build.gradle:254 whose next line reads `// Internal module so enforced platform dependencies are OK` (line 258). Resolved: zero enforcedPlatform violations exist in the corpus; spring-boot is a POSITIVE exemplar, and GRADLE-DEP-05's verification cell must say \"read the owning module\", not \"grep and flag\" — a raw-count rule flags the repo that gets it right.", "strictly/!! severity: declaration-and-catalogs proposed MUST but flagged its own grep as untested against the corpus and said it 'should be SHOULD until measured'. Measured today: 3 `strictly(` call sites (gradle/gradle build-platform:17, junit-framework junit4-compatibility:17, detekt-cli:26 — all build-logic/compat/CLI) and 98 `!!` catalog pins, all in gradle/gradle's distribution/test/provided catalogs. Resolved upward to MUST-with-scope: banned in a published library's own declarations, explicitly permitted in an application, distribution/test catalog, build-logic platform or CLI.", "Does platform alignment substitute for locking? declaration-and-catalogs candidate 5 accepts 'a lockfile OR an enforced platform' as satisfying 'pinned'; locking-verification candidate 1 makes locking a MUST outright. Resolved against declaration-and-catalogs: a platform constrains which versions are allowed for the coordinates it names and records nothing about what was resolved for the ones it does not, so it cannot detect drift. GRADLE-DEP-09's check is a committed lockfile, full stop.", "Locking severity: locking-verification's own Contested section narrows its candidate-1 MUST to 'MUST for the OCX SDK and Gradle plugin, SHOULD elsewhere'. Adopted, because 0/32 exemplars lock — making it a blanket MUST would flag every repo in the corpus including gradle/gradle.", "DAGP as a gate: declaration-and-catalogs recommended `issues { all { onAny { severity(\"fail\") } } }` while flagging corpus adoption as entirely unmeasured (its Contested #1). Measured today: 2/32 — gradle/gradle fails only on duplicate-class warnings (gradlebuild.root-build.gradle.kts:25), detekt applies build-health 3.19.1 (settings.gradle.kts:54). Resolved: the rule requires at least one failing category rather than `onAny`, since the corpus's strictest adopter deliberately narrowed; the exact set for the SDK is deferred to the owner (Q-DEP-3).", "The '28%' hallucination figure: the topic map's dive brief states 28%; locking-verification found Sonatype's real range is 7%–25%+ by model generation with 28% at the upper end, and recommends citing the range. Resolved by removing the number entirely from GRADLE-DEP-18 — the rule cites the mechanism (resolve before emitting), because a frontier-model agent reading a stale 25% discounts the rule, and the check is unconditional regardless of rate.", "dependencyConvergence vs requireUpperBoundDeps as the Maven default: maven-mediation-and-enforcer explicitly left both open (its Contested #1). Resolved: `requireUpperBoundDeps` is the universal floor (cheaper, fewer false positives in a large reactor, real adoption base in grpc-java's 8 example POMs) and `dependencyConvergence` is added only where two or more paths genuinely reach the same library (assertj's shape, 1/32).", "Maven 4 GA: the topic map's conflict 19 and frame correction 1 both hedged at rc-6 and asked wave 2 to settle it with a Central query. Settled by maven-mediation-and-enforcer's query on 2026-09-12: maven-core stable is 3.9.16, the 4.0 line tops out at 4.0.0-rc-6, no plain 4.0.0 exists. MVN-DEP-11 therefore bans bom packaging and consumer-POM flattening as shipped mechanism and carries the re-check command."], "needs_another_round": ["mavenLocal() and repository hygiene beyond content filtering — does the JVM rule set ban mavenLocal() outright, and at what severity, given 42 build files across the corpus declare it? No dive covered it, Gradle's Best Practices page naming it was not fetched for this family, so no rule was written — yet it is the second-most-likely thing an agent adds to 'fix' a resolution failure after a version bump, and it silently makes a build machine-dependent.", "Maven-side checksum and verification policy — what is the Maven equivalent of verification-metadata.xml, and does the aether checksum-algorithm list harden or weaken a build? The corpus's only checksum configuration (assertj@485502bad2:.mvn/maven.config) sets `-Daether.checksums.algorithms=SHA-512,SHA-256,SHA-1,MD5 -Daether.connector.smartChecksums=false`, enumerating two algorithms Gradle's docs call insufficient alongside two secure ones; no source read here settles which it is. MVN-DEP currently has no supply-chain-integrity row at all while GRADLE-DEP has three.", "Dependency-update automation as a rule surface — does the rule set say anything about Dependabot vs Renovate configuration, given 13/32 each with zero overlap and an agent is the thing reviewing their PRs? The publishing audit measured the split; no dive turned it into guidance, and GRADLE-DEP-18 (resolve before emitting) has an unexamined interaction with a bot that proposes bumps automatically."]}, "rule_id_count": 29, "dives": [{"slug": "declaration-and-catalogs", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-dependencies/declaration-and-catalogs.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["gradle/gradle — the tool that invented version catalogs — does not use the conventional `gradle/libs.versions.toml` path for its own build at all; it wires three named catalogs (libs/testLibs/buildLibs) programmatically, a blind spot for any filename-only glob rule.", "spring-boot, a library, is the corpus's ONLY enforcedPlatform() user (13 hits) — the exact anti-pattern Gradle's own docs warn a reusable component against, shipped by one of the most widely-depended-on Java libraries in existence.", "0/32 exemplars use dependency locking at all, so 'version catalog' and 'pinned versions' are conflated everywhere in practice even though Gradle's own docs explicitly distinguish declared vs resolved.", "No wave-1 audit or this dive could confirm any of the 32 exemplars actually apply DAGP (`com.autonomousapps.dependency-analysis`) to themselves — the tool this brief centers on is undermeasured in the very corpus used to justify it.", "DAGP's own README is AsciiDoc, not Markdown, and its FAQ wiki page does not define the four advice categories (unused/wrong-configuration/transitive/annotation-processor) at all — those live only on the wiki's Home page, a routing trap for anyone fetching just the README or just the FAQ as the brief's phrasing implies."]}, {"slug": "locking-verification-supply-chain", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-dependencies/locking-verification-supply-chain.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["The one gradle.lockfile hit in the entire 32-repo corpus is gradle/gradle's own documentation snippet demonstrating the feature to readers, not a real lock on any build — locking has zero genuine exemplar support anywhere in the corpus.", "Maven Central's namespace verification is what let the Jackson prefix-swap attack (org.fasterxml.jackson.core vs real com.fasterxml.jackson.core) through legitimately: DNS verification is domain-exact, so the attacker registering fasterxml.org passed verification for the org. namespace on its own merits — verification worked correctly and was still the wrong control.", "Only gradle/gradle itself (1/32) has verification-metadata.xml among 32 flagship JVM exemplars including major libraries like okhttp, guava, and jackson-databind.", "Sonatype's actual per-model hallucination rates span 7%-25%+ depending on generation (older models ~1-in-4, 2026 frontier models ~7-8%) — the brief's flat '28%' is the upper/older-model end, not a single stable figure, and is dropping fast.", "Gradle's own docs state the bootstrap command's trust limitation in almost the exact words a security review would use ('trusts whatever is currently in your repositories'), making 'run the bootstrap command' insufficient as a rule on its own — it needs a mandatory reviewed-diff step attached.", "A failed Gradle build silently discards any --write-locks changes, so a naive CI script chaining --write-locks with the main build can mask lockfile drift as if nothing happened."]}, {"slug": "maven-mediation-and-enforcer", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-dependencies/maven-mediation-and-enforcer.md", "sources_count": 17, "surprises": ["Maven 4.0.0 is confirmed still RC as of 2026-09-12: Central's index tops out at maven-core 4.0.0-rc-6, with 3.9.16 the latest stable — bom packaging and consumer-POM flattening are migration-preview only, not adoptable yet.", "grpc-java binds requireUpperBoundDeps in 8 examples/*/pom.xml files (enforcer 3.5.0) — a real, un-briefed adoption data point for that rule beyond assertj/guava.", "The ASF parent POM (v39) enforces only Maven/Java version floors and a signed reproducible-release profile — zero dependency-drift protection, which falsifies the assumption that a strict widely-inherited parent already covers convergence/duplicate checks.", "Maven's own docs flag that dependencyManagement can silently downgrade an unrelated dependency sharing coordinates — a documented footgun distinct from the mediation-vs-management confusion the brief anticipated.", "Version range gotcha confirmed verbatim from primary docs: `[1.0,2.0)` includes `2.0-rc1` because '2.0-rc1' sorts below '2.0' — contrary to most users' expectation.", "assertj and guava both pin every plugin version manually in pluginManagement but neither binds requirePluginVersions itself (0/32) — manual discipline with zero enforcer backstop is the norm, not the exception."]}]}, {"group": "distribution", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-distribution.md", "must_count": 15, "conflicts_resolved": ["\"Zero counterexamples in 32 repos: no consumable library shades\" (topic-map conflict 1, pub audit finding 4, BOTH dives) vs. measured reality — RESOLVED AGAINST the claim. Re-read apache__kafka@940c100fab:build.gradle:2089-2100 (project(':clients') shadowJar, archiveClassifier = null) and :401-420 (publishes components.shadow as the mavenJava publication): kafka-clients, the corpus's most-declared library artifact, IS a shaded jar under primary coordinates. The audit's Axis-2 'who consumes' table mis-filed it as 'build-tooling submodule, not kafka-clients'. DIST-02 keeps MUST status but ships with a three-condition carve-out (every bundled package relocated under the library's own namespace; non-vendored deps left as real POM deps; reason recorded) instead of an absolute.", "error-prone relocation: exemplar-publishing-ci-bazel.md Axis 2 (`<relocation>` = 1, 'yes (<relocation>)') and topic-map row M-G-04 say error-prone relocates 'because it loads into javac'; shading-failure-taxonomy.md finding 8 says it does not. RESOLVED FOR THE TAXONOMY, re-verified at the pinned SHA: google__error-prone@c1f99ad5d3:core/pom.xml:403-413 has artifactSet/shadedClassifierName/createDependencyReducedPom and NO <relocations> block; the repo's only <relocation> is type_annotations/pom.xml:31-34, a <distributionManagement> artifact-move notice. The audit count is a grep false positive. Ships as AI-agent failure mode 9 and a grep trap in DIST-11.", "Does Gradle sidestep Maven's dependency-reduced-POM trade-off? shading-failure-taxonomy.md finding 10 says yes ('Gradle's publication model lets a shaded and unshaded artifact each carry independently correct dependency metadata without this trade-off'). RESOLVED AGAINST IT: apache__kafka@940c100fab:build.gradle:410-420 hand-rolls a 20-line pom.withXml loop against GradleUp/shadow#324 to re-add the non-bundled deps. DIST-13 therefore binds both build systems symmetrically, and the residual 'what does components.shadow actually emit on Shadow 9.6.1' is flagged for another round.", "Spring Boot + Shadow co-application: distribution-shape-decision.md candidate 4 states it as a MUST; shading-failure-taxonomy.md candidate 11 softens it to 'question why both are needed'. RESOLVED FOR THE MUST (DIST-16) — the more specific formulation, and the check is a two-grep set intersection with an unambiguous defect signature.", "minimize() severity: the taxonomy debates whether it is recommendable at all. RESOLVED as a conditional MUST (DIST-10) rather than a ban, on re-measured evidence that 0/32 exemplars use it — the corpus's restraint means a ban would flag nothing and the conditional catches the one case an agent introduces."], "needs_another_round": ["Shaded-library publication metadata under Gradle — On Shadow >= 9.0.0, does publishing `components.shadow` produce a POM whose <dependencies> correctly equal the non-bundled runtime set without hand-patching, and from which version? (kafka still hand-patches via pom.withXml against shadow#324; the GRADLE-PUB half of the question — what a Maven consumer sees through Gradle Module Metadata — is topic-map row M-H-02.)", "minimize() in 2026 — Has jdependency-backed minimization gained any ServiceLoader / META-INF/services awareness since Shadow 9.0.0, and does minimize() interact correctly with mergeServiceFiles() on a current version? Every claim in this family traces to one sentence of Shadow's docs; 0/32 exemplars exercise it, so nothing is corroborated.", "Reproducible shaded archives (straddles GRADLE-DIST and GRADLE-CACHE) — Gradle 9 makes archive tasks reproducible by default (map row M-D-11), but is a shadowJar output byte-identical across two clean builds on Shadow 9.6.1 / Gradle 9.7, and does mergeServiceFiles() preserve entry order? Neither dive checked whether shadowJar inherits the default."]}, "rule_id_count": 20, "dives": [{"slug": "shading-failure-taxonomy", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-distribution/shading-failure-taxonomy.md", "sources_count": 18, "surprises": ["google/error-prone does NOT relocate packages when shading, contradicting the wave-1 topic map's conflict-1 claim — its core/pom.xml maven-shade-plugin execution only restricts artifactSet includes, with createDependencyReducedPom=false and no <relocations> block at all; the one <relocation> grep-hit in the repo is an unrelated distributionManagement artifact-move notice in type_annotations/pom.xml", "Maven Shade's dependency-reduced POM defaults to true and is required only when the shaded jar publishes under normal (non-classified) coordinates; error-prone explicitly sets it to false because it ships a classified with-dependencies jar alongside an unshaded primary artifact — a real, code-backed answer to the brief's decision (c)", "jmh-gradle-plugin's own current upstream fix for the signed-jar SecurityException omits .EC (ECDSA signature files) from its exclude list — the most commonly copy-pasted reference fix has this gap", "grpc-java's and dagger's preserveFileTimestamps grep hits are confirmed (via the pub audit) to be a hand-written Transformer method parameter name, not the Gradle archive property — a durable grep trap worth naming explicitly in the depth file", "Shadow has no built-in transformer for Spring's spring.factories as of today (GradleUp/shadow#1489 still open) even though the shape of the fix is well understood by the maintainers and multiple third parties have hand-rolled SpringFileTransformer implementations", "No exemplar in the 32-repo corpus was found actually using minimize() at all — the caution around it is sourced entirely from Shadow's own docs and issue history, not corroborated (or contradicted) by real exemplar usage"]}, {"slug": "distribution-shape-decision", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-distribution/distribution-shape-decision.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["The YouTrack SUPPORT-A-2700 article body is a client-rendered SPA that couldn't be scraped via WebFetch or curl — had to corroborate its content via an independent tacosteemers.com walkthrough and cite YouTrack as topic-only confirmation", "jpackage has macOS signing flags but genuinely no notarization step of its own per the official spec — notarization is always a separate xcrun notarytool invocation, worth its own MUST", "Jib's own layer split (other deps → snapshot deps → project deps → resources → classes → extra dirs) is finer-grained than Spring Boot's 4-layer order but follows the identical stable-to-volatile principle", "Application-plugin usage (7/32) and Spring Boot's 383 bootJar hits both skew hard to sample/smoke-test modules, not shipped executables — citing either raw count without the per-module caveat would mislead", "JEP 493 (jlink without JMODs) is a JDK-vendor opt-in build flag, not a universal JDK 24+ guarantee — no cross-linking, and an image built this way can't itself include jdk.jlink", "0/32 exemplars use jlink or jpackage at all, so those two sections are precondition-derived from primary docs rather than corpus-corroborated — flagged explicitly in the file rather than presented as measured practice"]}]}, {"group": "quality-gates", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-quality-gates.md", "must_count": 15, "conflicts_resolved": ["Coverage enforcement count — the map's conflict 11 and the gates audit say 3/32 enforce a numeric floor; test-suite-and-coverage.md shows two of those three gate their own gate. Resolved for the sub-artifact (it read the surrounding conditionals; the audit greppped for the construct): 3/32 DECLARE a floor, exactly 1/32 (assertj) enforces it unconditionally. Split into JAVA-TEST-08 (construct present) and JAVA-TEST-09 (read the enclosing conditional), because a presence-only check misreports junit-framework and kotlinx.coroutines.", "StringCaseLocaleUsage's set membership — map row M-R-01 frames all nine promotable checks as DISABLED_CHECKS members; java-lint-gate.md's direct count against BuiltInCheckerSuppliers.java finds it in ENABLED_WARNINGS (line 1177, inside the 918-1236 range). Resolved for the source read over the map's summary: the switch is a severity bump (-Xep:StringCaseLocaleUsage:ERROR), not an activation. A rule saying 'enable' it would ask an agent to do nothing.", "Error Prone + NullAway as 'the gate of record' vs 19/32 running neither — the map's conflict 9 declares the gate of record without a severity; the gates audit measures Error Prone 13/32 and NullAway 4/32. Resolved by splitting severity per consumer kind: SHOULD for an adopter (a MUST would flag 19 flagships), MUST for the OCX SDK and OCX Gradle plugin, which are green-field.", "SpotBugs pairing with find-sec-bugs — java-lint-gate.md flagged its own open question ('corpus does not confirm how many of those 3 also wire find-sec-bugs'). Re-measured 2026-09-12 across all 32 clones: find-sec-bugs is 0/32, so all three SpotBugs gates (kafka, assertj, spotless) violate the rule. Resolved in favour of the rule with the violation stated rather than softening it, and the residual 'is plain SpotBugs earning anything' question escalated to another round.", "junit-platform.properties key spelling — both wave-1 audits and the sub-artifact's brief print keys in the suffix form (execution.parallel.enabled). Verified verbatim by git show against micronaut-core@d5842045bb and mockito@5a676bcd9e: the real files carry the junit.jupiter. prefix. Resolved for the files: the audits' tables are shorthand, and a grep written from them matches nothing. Recorded as a correction inside JAVA-TEST-03.", "Checkstyle stock-ruleset severity — java-lint-gate.md's candidate 9 makes a byte-identical google_checks.xml copy a finding, which would flag okhttp, the corpus's one stock user. Reading okhttp's convention plugin (2026-09-12) sharpens it: it never copies the file at all, it resolves it from the Checkstyle jar via resources.text.fromArchiveEntry, so the gate moves on every toolVersion bump with no diff. Resolved as MUST on 'committed file' and SHOULD on 'pruned, not stock' — two claims, two severities.", "Kover's default check wiring — test-suite-and-coverage.md infers from one convention-plugin comment that koverVerify auto-binds to check, the opposite of JaCoCo, while noting Kover's own docs never say so. Resolved by declining to write a rule on it: JAVA-TEST-08 requires the explicit wire regardless of tool, which is correct under either default, and the question is escalated to a KT-TEST round.", "Surefire 3.6.0 GA status — the map's conflict 20 resolves 'both scouts are right about different things'; test-suite-and-coverage.md queried Central directly and found 3.6.0-M1 (2026-06-02) newest, 3.5.6 the newest GA line. Resolved for the registry query over the vendor doc banner, and generalised into JAVA-TEST-12 as a method rule, not just a version fact.", "Whether the coverage gate is the OCX Gradle plugin's gate at all — map conflict 12 sets MUST-for-SDK / CONSIDER-for-adopter but is silent on the plugin. Resolved: a percentage measures the wrong thing for an artifact whose failure mode is 'breaks on Gradle 9.7'; the plugin's gate is the TestKit cross-version matrix (owned by GRADLE-PLUG), and JAVA-TEST-10 is scoped to exclude it explicitly so nobody specifies a coverage number there."], "needs_another_round": ["KT-TEST — Does Kover's koverVerify bind to `check` by default? Kover 0.9.8's own docs never state it; the only evidence is a comment in kotlinx.coroutines' kover-conventions.gradle.kts implying it does, which is the opposite default from JaCoCo. No KT-TEST coverage rule can be written either way until a pass fetches Kover's DSL reference or reads the plugin source.", "JAVA-LINT — With find-sec-bugs at 0/32, does plain SpotBugs earn anything on a project already running Error Prone + NullAway? All three corpus SpotBugs gates run it without the plugin, so either they see something the rule cannot, or three flagships run a redundant slow pass. Settling it needs a diff of SpotBugs' CORRECTNESS/MT_CORRECTNESS patterns against Error Prone's ENABLED_ERRORS — attempted by nobody in waves 1 or 2.", "JAVA-TEST — Which JUnit release ships which @ResourceLock capability (target = CHILDREN, ResourceLocksProvider, Vintage-engine parallel method execution)? Four merged PRs are named but never mapped to versions, so JAVA-TEST-05 has to say 'carry the PR you verified against' instead of 'available from 6.x'."]}, "rule_id_count": 23, "dives": [{"slug": "java-lint-gate", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-quality-gates/java-lint-gate.md", "sources_count": 17, "surprises": ["StringCaseLocaleUsage, which the brief listed among the 'disabled checks worth promoting,' is actually already in ENABLED_WARNINGS, not DISABLED_CHECKS — verified by direct line count against BuiltInCheckerSuppliers.java. 'Promoting' it means a severity bump, not activation from off.", "NullAway's -XDaddTypeAnnotationsToSymbol=true workaround for JDK 17.0.19+/21.0.8+ explicitly does NOT work on Oracle JDK builds — only OpenJDK-family distributions (Temurin, Zulu) — a toolchain-vendor trap, not just a version trap.", "PMD is not merely under-adopted but actively performative in the flagship corpus: apache/maven declares maven-pmd-plugin with zero bound <executions>, and gradle/gradle ships the Gradle PMD plugin to users without ever running it on its own build.", "jackson-databind's Error Prone usage is an opt-in Maven <profile>, not the default build — the most-cited 'lenient pole' exemplar isn't even running Error Prone by default, only when a developer explicitly activates the profile.", "SpotBugs' own SECURITY category (1 of 10) is deliberately thin by SpotBugs' own design intent, not an oversight later patched by find-sec-bugs — the two were always meant to be paired.", "Four incompatible Java nullness annotation systems (jsr305, Checker Framework, JSpecify declared, NullAway JSpecify-native) coexist simultaneously in the measured corpus with no dominant winner yet — Guava 33.4.1+ shipping @NullMarked means any NullAway user inherits stricter checking at Guava call sites regardless of their own project's mode."]}, {"slug": "test-suite-and-coverage", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-quality-gates/test-suite-and-coverage.md", "sources_count": 16, "surprises": ["kotlinx.coroutines' Kover minBound(85) — one of the map's three 'enforced' exemplars — does NOT run on a bare `./gradlew check`; it's gated behind `-Pkover.enabled=true` in a separate CI step, so the true unconditional-enforcement count is closer to 1/32 (assertj) than 3/32", "Maven Central has no GA maven-surefire-plugin:3.6.0 as of 2026-09-12 — the plugin's own docs site header reads 'Version: 3.6.0, Last Published: 2026-08-31' as if it shipped, but the newest artifact on Central is 3.6.0-M1 (2026-06-02); an initial WebFetch pass on the docs page itself hallucinated 'Release Type: GA', caught only by querying Central directly", "junit-platform-runner's removal has NO replacement, but a web-search snippet (not the primary wiki page) claimed its content 'is now included directly in junit-platform-launcher' — conflating it with the separate junit-platform-jfr removal, which does fold into junit-platform-launcher; only fetching the primary wiki page caught this", "Gradle's own docs state jacocoTestCoverageVerification is explicitly NOT a dependency of check — meaning a project can have a fully-configured violationRules block with a real threshold and it will never fail a CI build unless someone adds `check.dependsOn(...)`, a wiring gap distinct from 'coverage tool applied vs not'", "micronaut-core sets `execution.parallel.enabled = true` and nothing else in its junit-platform.properties — a live, real-world instance of the exact half-configuration trap (enabled with no mode.default) that makes parallel execution silently still sequential", "@ResourceLock/@Isolated is not a stable, finished feature: junit-team/junit-framework merged four PRs in 2025-2026 changing its semantics (child-node propagation, programmatic ResourceLocksProvider, Vintage-engine parallel methods) — a rule citing a specific capability here needs a version/PR citation the way StructuredTaskScope rows do"]}]}, {"group": "language-api", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-language-api.md", "must_count": 19, "conflicts_resolved": ["abiValidation{} is 2/32, not 0/32 — re-measured this session: pinterest__ktlint@4c933394a3:build-logic/src/main/kotlin/KotlinCommonPlugin.kt:38-39 and detekt__detekt@45672efb8b:build-logic/src/main/kotlin/public-api.gradle.kts:13-14 both call abiValidation() under @OptIn(ExperimentalAbiValidation::class), resolving gates §1's 'BCV wiring not locatable' flag. Standalone BCV stays the default (no Experimental opt-in) but the 'no working counter-example' rationale is withdrawn and KT-API-03 becomes 'run exactly one', not a prohibition.", "explicit-API-mode adopter set: shape says 5/25 (incl. ktlint, excl. kotlinx.coroutines), gates says 5/32 (incl. kotlinx.coroutines, excl. ktlint). Each grep missed a form — shape missed the -Xexplicit-api=strict compiler flag, gates missed a call living in a build-logic .kt convention-plugin class. Union is 6/32; the consequence is KT-API-01's check must grep three spellings across *.gradle.kts, *.gradle AND build-logic/**/*.kt.", "gradle/gradle's binary-compat mechanism: gates calls it a bespoke Groovy checker 'not japicmp/revapi'; the java dive read the class and found JapicmpTaskWithKotlin extends me.champeau.gradle.japicmp.JapicmpTask. Resolved for the dive (source read beats filename inference) — japicmp is the corpus's convergence point, and the bespoke JSON allowlist is a pattern to layer on it, not a competitor.", "M-K-02's 'carry forRemoval and since' vs JEP 277's 'forRemoval only with a definite plan': resolved on the normative source — since is unconditional, omitting forRemoval is the correct spelling of an ordinary deprecation, forRemoval=true needs a removal version already in the Javadoc. Merged into one rule (JAVA-API-05) so the halves cannot drift.", "The java dive's table marks 'add an abstract method to an interface' as having 'no runtime effect for callers'. Resolved against JLS §13.5.3: linkage survives but invoking the method on a pre-existing implementation throws AbstractMethodError — hence JAVA-API-03 is a MUST for a default body, not a footnote.", "Kotlin dive candidate 3 says 'never take a bare Boolean' while its own §8 carves out a fluent single-setter. Resolved toward the carve-out with the severity following it: KT-API-10 is SHOULD, because a MUST shipped with a documented exception teaches agents that every MUST is negotiable.", "japicmp vs revapi: revapi has the better design and 0/32 adoption. Resolved on measured practice over design — japicmp, with its cost stated and the decision flagged for re-taking rather than settled.", "JSpecify direction vs installed base (map conflict 8) applied: severity split — MUST for new published surface and the OCX SDK, SHOULD with a coexistence note for an existing one; the unconditional half is JAVA-API-10 'never mix flavours in one file', which contradicts none of the corpus."], "needs_another_round": ["Source-level public-API measurement (JAVA-API, KT-API) — every wave-1 audit measured config files only. Which of JAVA-API-04..11 and KT-API-04..11 do the strict exemplars actually satisfy in Java/Kotlin source (public data class, Optional in parameter position, @Deprecated without since, misplaced @Nullable array annotations, default on a sealed switch), as opposed to in build configuration? Six rows are currently 'not independently measured' in both dives.", "japicmp configuration depth (JAVA-API) — are the six 'declared' japicmp repos (apache/maven, assertj, gradle/gradle, grpc-java, micronaut-core, testcontainers-java) actually build-blocking, and what does an accepted-breaks file look like outside gradle/gradle's Groovy scaffolding? JAVA-API-02 has exactly one template and it is wrapped in machinery no adopter will copy.", "revapi revisit (JAVA-API) — has revapi acquired any adopter or a maintained Gradle plugin since 0.15.1? The japicmp decision rests entirely on 0/32 adoption, not on design, so it should be re-taken rather than inherited if that changes."]}, "rule_id_count": 26, "dives": [{"slug": "java-api-evolution-and-nullness", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-language-api/java-api-evolution-and-nullness.md", "sources_count": 17, "surprises": ["gradle/gradle's 'bespoke' binary-compatibility mechanism is not from-scratch — JapicmpTaskWithKotlin.java literally extends me.champeau.gradle.japicmp.JapicmpTask; it's japicmp plus a reviewed JSON allowlist (accepted-public-api-changes.json), not an independent tool, contrary to how the wave-1 audit phrased it", "Adding a new overload is binary-*compatible* by JLS (old binaries keep resolving to the old signature) even though it can silently redirect which overload *new* source compilations pick — a source-compat, not binary-compat, hazard that cuts the opposite way from intuition", "Changing a static final constant's value never breaks linkage — un-recompiled callers just silently keep the stale inlined value forever, with no error signal at all", "guava proves module-info.java and Java-8 bytecode are not mutually exclusive: the base build still uses the deprecated <source>1.8</source>/<target>1.8</target> pair while module-info.java lives in a separate MR-JAR layer compiled with <release>9</release> — a pattern worth naming explicitly", "The 2024 DOP v1.1 essay silently replaced its own fourth principle: 'validate at the boundary' (2022) became 'separate operations from data' — citing the 2022 four principles today cites withdrawn guidance", "revapi is architecturally the better-designed tool (pluggable analyzers, first-class intentional-change marking) yet has zero adoption anywhere in the 32-repo corpus, so recommending it would be a bet against all observed practice rather than a codification of it"]}, {"slug": "kotlin-api-and-abi", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-language-api/kotlin-api-and-abi.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["Narrowing a return type breaks binary compatibility exactly like widening does — it looks source-safe (even type-safe) and still throws NoSuchMethodError because the JVM call site is bound to the exact erased descriptor", "Adding a value to an annotation's @Target set can silently redirect Java-reflection lookups from a field to the compiler-preferred property target, with zero Kotlin-side symptom and no compile error", "@JvmOverloads is Java-callers-only and does nothing for Kotlin callers — the only mechanism that fixes both (@IntroducedAt under ExperimentalVersionOverloading) is itself still Experimental, so there is no fully-solved, stable answer to 'add a default parameter without breaking Kotlin callers' today", "Two of Kotlin's three official library rules (visibility, explicit types) are fully subsumed by explicitApi() as compiler errors, but the third (KDoc every public member) has no compiler-level gate at all — only a Dokka report", "ktlint and detekt both carry substantial .api BCV dump files (17 and 8) with no locatable plugin application in the sparse-checked-out corpus slice — their BCV wiring lives in an unmaterialized build-logic file, so they can't be cited as confirmed standalone-BCV adopters, only as artifact evidence", "ktor's explicitApi = null is a per-module opt-in switch, not a repo-wide policy disagreement with kotlinlang's guidance — a naive grep for the setting misreads it as ktor rejecting explicit API mode"]}]}]
const WAVE3_STAGED = [{"group": "concurrency", "group_label": "Concurrency: the correctness surface agents get wrong most", "id_family": "JAVA-CONC", "slug": "java-virtual-threads-and-structured-concurrency", "label": "Virtual threads after JDK 24, and a preview API that will not stand still", "brief": "Write the Java concurrency rules with a JDK number on every version-sensitive\nrow, and settle what a rule may say about an API in its seventh preview.\n\nFetch, primary only: openjdk.org/jeps/444 (virtual threads, final JDK 21),\n/491 (synchronize without pinning, JDK 24), /506 (scoped values, final JDK 25),\n/525 (structured concurrency 6th preview, JDK 26), /533 (7th preview, JDK 27,\nGA 2026-09-15); the JDK 25 javadoc for StructuredTaskScope and\njava.util.concurrent; jcip.net/contents.html for the pre-Loom baseline.\n\nPin down: JEP 444's two absolutes (\"a new virtual thread should be created for\nevery application task\", \"should never be pooled\"); exactly what JEP 491 fixed in\nJDK 24 (monitors acquired, held and released independently of the carrier, so\nsynchronized/wait/notify no longer pin) and exactly what it did not (blocking\nwhile resolving a class reference, blocking inside a class initializer, waiting\non another thread's class initialization); that jdk.tracePinnedThreads was\nREMOVED in JDK 24 and setting it now does nothing, leaving the\njdk.VirtualThreadPinned JFR event as the only detector; ScopedValue's finalised\nshape including that orElse no longer accepts null; and the full\nStructuredTaskScope changelog across previews — fork() returning Subtask not\nFuture, constructors replaced by open()/open(Joiner), Joiner's built-in\nfactories, allSuccessfulOrThrow() changing from a stream to a list, onTimeout()\nadded then renamed to timeout() with CancelledByTimeoutException, a third type\nparameter for the thrown exception type, awaitAll() removed.\n\nDECIDE: (a) the exact wording of the synchronized-versus-ReentrantLock rule for\na project whose floor is JDK 17 or 21 versus one at 24+, because a single\nunconditional sentence is wrong for one of them; (b) whether shipped code may\nuse StructuredTaskScope at all, and if so under what guard; (c) the rule for\nbounding fan-out when virtual threads have removed the platform-thread ceiling\nthat used to throttle it accidentally, naming the downstream resources that\nstill have limits (connection pools, native libraries, rate-limited APIs).\n\nTest against: jackson-core#919 is a flagship library auditing its own\nThreadLocal-based parser/buffer pooling ahead of virtual threads — use it as the\nworked example for why per-thread caching fights the model. micronaut-core is\nthe corpus's only --enable-preview user (1/32), so preview-flag usage is not a\ncorpus norm and a rule recommending one must say so."}, {"group": "concurrency", "group_label": "Concurrency: the correctness surface agents get wrong most", "id_family": "KT-CORO", "slug": "kotlin-coroutines-correctness", "label": "Where a coroutine loses an exception or refuses to stop", "brief": "Write the coroutine rules from JetBrains' own wrong/right pairs, so each row\nnames a mistake rather than a principle.\n\nFetch, primary only: kotlinlang.org/docs/exception-handling.html,\n/coroutine-context-and-dispatchers.html, /shared-mutable-state-and-concurrency.html,\n/cancellation-and-timeouts.html; the kotlinx-coroutines-test README; the\nDispatchers KDoc; elizarov.medium.com \"Kotlin and Exceptions\" and \"Structured\nConcurrency\" for the design rationale.\n\nPin down: that CoroutineExceptionHandler is consulted only on a root coroutine\n(one created directly in a CoroutineScope/supervisorScope, or a direct child of\nSupervisorJob) and has NO effect on async, whose exception surfaces only at\nawait(); that supervisorScope makes each direct child behave as an independent\nroot, which is not the same as being a child of a SupervisorJob; that\nCancellationException is transparent to handlers by design and must be rethrown,\nnever swallowed by catch(Exception) or runCatching; the first-exception-wins\nplus .suppressed aggregation rule; Dispatchers.IO's ceiling (64 threads or the\ncore count, whichever is larger, via kotlinx.coroutines.io.parallelism) and that\nlimitedParallelism(n) yields an elastic view reusing IO's own threads rather\nthan allocating new ones, so views do not sum; and runTest's three properties\n(auto delay-skip, 60-second default timeout, end-of-test propagation of uncaught\nchild exceptions) plus StandardTestDispatcher versus UnconfinedTestDispatcher.\n\nDECIDE: (a) the MUST list, five rows or fewer, each greppable — GlobalScope,\nrunBlocking inside suspend, handler on a non-root coroutine, swallowed\nCancellationException, unbounded blocking work on Dispatchers.IO; (b) whether\n\"never use GlobalScope\" is absolute or carries a named exception; (c) how a\nreview skill detects a missing cooperative-cancellation check in a long-running\nloop, given there is no lint for it.\n\nAlso settle the corpus's one open Kotlin-testing question: whether\nrunTest's virtual time can hide a real suspension-point race that only appears\non a real dispatcher, and if so what a test must do about it."}, {"group": "concurrency", "group_label": "Concurrency: the correctness surface agents get wrong most", "id_family": "JAVA-CONC", "slug": "process-and-cli-boundary", "label": "Wrapping a CLI: the SDK's actual concurrency surface — REVISE after wave 2 and owner Q1", "brief": "Design the process boundary for a JVM SDK that wraps a CLI one command at a\ntime, by translating ocx-sdk-python's measured contract rather than inventing one.\n\nRead first, as the specification: ocx-sdk-python/src/ocx_sdk/_process.py:101-105,\n383-421, 444-457, 762-799 (injectable PopenFactory/ExecFactory seams for the sync\nand async paths, one-shot run_command, spawn, a kill ladder) and\n_errors.py:28-46 (the 16-member ExitCode IntEnum mapped to an exception\nhierarchy, never parsed from stderr text); rules_ocx/AGENTS.md:103-159 (the\ntwo-tier CLI JSON contract), :161-178 (14 sysexits), :66-72 (every reachable\nsysexit maps to a failure naming the fix, and 75 is retried before failing), and\n:211-249 (OCX_ENV_CLASSES: 10 site, 4 translucent, 2 explicit, 5 pinned).\n\nFetch, primary only: the ProcessBuilder and Process javadoc for JDK 25\n(destroy/destroyForcibly, waitFor(timeout), onExit, the redirect and\nenvironment surfaces); docs.gradle.org ExecOperations and ValueSource; the\njava.net.http HttpClient javadoc if the zero-dependency JSON question is live.\n\nPin down: how a JVM caller reads stdout and stderr concurrently without\ndeadlocking on a full pipe buffer; what a kill ladder looks like on the JVM\n(destroy, wait, destroyForcibly, wait, report) and what each step guarantees on\nPOSIX and on Windows; how the child's environment is built explicitly rather\nthan inherited, matching the four OCX_ENV_CLASSES; and how the same boundary is\nexpressed on the Gradle-plugin side through ExecOperations under\nconfiguration-cache rules.\n\nDECIDE: (a) the seam — an interface with a constructor-injected factory, since\nJava has no mockable process API; state it as a design MUST for the SDK;\n(b) whether the async path uses virtual threads, Process.onExit's\nCompletableFuture, or coroutines, and how that survives the Java-first/Kotlin-first\ndecision either way; (c) the retry rule for sysexit 75 and where it lives so it\ncannot be forgotten; (d) whether the exit-code enum is generated from a shared\nsource or hand-maintained against the two prior-art files, and what keeps it\nbyte-identical."}, {"group": "publishing", "group_label": "Publishing: getting an artifact out, and keeping consumers working", "id_family": "GRADLE-PUB", "slug": "central-portal-and-provenance", "label": "The Central Portal runbook, and how far provenance has actually got", "brief": "Produce the release runbook that becomes the jvm-release skill, with every\nrequirement stated as a gate rather than a step.\n\nFetch, primary only: central.sonatype.org/publish/publish-portal-guide/,\n/publish/requirements/, /publish/publish-portal-maven/, /register/central-portal/,\n/pages/ossrh-eol/ and /news/20250326_ossrh_sunset/; the vanniktech\ngradle-maven-publish-plugin and com.gradleup.nmcp READMEs; slsa.dev/spec/v1.1/levels;\nthe Sigstore-validation announcement dated 2025-01-28.\n\nPin down: namespace verification as a mandatory precondition and how it is\nproven (DNS TXT, or GitHub repo ownership for io.github.*); the per-artifact\nrequirement set (sources jar, javadoc jar, a GPG/PGP signature per file, MD5 and\nSHA1 checksums, a POM carrying name/description/url/licenses/developers/scm);\nautoPublish and waitUntil (validated | uploaded | published) and what each\nmeans for a CI job's exit; that SNAPSHOT publishing needs\ncentral-publishing-maven-plugin >= 0.7.0; the OSSRH Staging API compatibility\nshim as a bridge, not a target; and immutability as an absolute.\n\nThen place the supply-chain bar honestly. Sigstore bundles are validated at the\nPortal since 2025-01-28 but not required. Across all 32 exemplars, uses of\nactions/attest-build-provenance, sigstore/cosign, dependency-review-action and\nany container or dependency scanner are ZERO — including in bazelbuild/bazel and\ngradle/gradle themselves. Establish which SLSA build level a normal GitHub\nActions release reaches, and state whether provenance attestation is a\nforward-looking rule this program introduces deliberately or a practice it\ncodifies. It is not the latter; say so.\n\nDECIDE: (a) the ordered gate list for the runbook, with the immutability\nconsequence stated first; (b) whether the tag-versus-declared-version check is a\nMUST (ocx-sdk-python already does it); (c) whether Sigstore signing is a\nSHOULD or a CONSIDER for the OCX SDK's first release."}, {"group": "publishing", "group_label": "Publishing: getting an artifact out, and keeping consumers working", "id_family": "GRADLE-PUB", "slug": "consumability-across-build-systems", "label": "What a Maven, Gradle and Bazel consumer each see — REVISE after wave 2", "brief": "Settle what a library published from one build system looks like to consumers on\nthe other two, so the OCX SDK is consumable everywhere it is claimed to be.\n\nFetch, primary only: docs.gradle.org/current/userguide/publishing_gradle_module_metadata.html,\n/publishing_maven.html, /java_platform_plugin.html; maven.apache.org/pom.html\n(the consumer's view) and whatsnewinmaven4.html (build POM versus consumer POM);\nbazel-contrib/rules_jvm_external README (java_export, maven.install boms,\nversion_conflict_policy).\n\nPin down: that Gradle Module Metadata is invisible to Maven by design — a Maven\nconsumer sees only the legacy POM, with a marker comment it cannot act on — so\nany Gradle-only variant (a shadowJar publication, a feature variant, a KMP\ntarget) simply does not exist for it and must be republished as a classified\nartifact if it matters; what Maven 4's consumer-POM flattening changes about\nwhat is published versus what is in source control, and that it is opt-in via\nmaven.consumer.pom.flatten=true; and how a Bazel consumer pins a Maven-published\nartifact through maven_install.json regardless of what produced it.\n\nDECIDE: (a) whether the OCX SDK suppresses GMM publishing or keeps it, and what\neach choice costs; (b) whether a Maven-consumability verification module is\nrequired — the corpus's own pattern, since dagger, grpc-java, okhttp, Exposed and\njunit-framework all ship a small maven-tests/examples/maven module that proves\nthe published POM resolves, rather than carrying a second build system; (c) what\nthe SDK's published POM must declare that Gradle does not generate by default.\n\nTest against: 0 of the 10 Gradle-tagged repos carrying a pom.xml build with both\nsystems; every incidental pom is either a Maven-consumability example\n(dagger examples/maven, grpc-java examples/*, okhttp maven-tests, Exposed,\njunit-framework tooling-support-tests) or a plugin's own integration-test fixture\n(spring-boot's 170, gradle/gradle's 46). The \"dual build system\" reading of those\nrepos is a false positive; the real pattern is a consumability test."}, {"group": "bazel-java", "group_label": "Bazel: the file the sibling set does not have", "id_family": "BZL-JAVA", "slug": "bazel-java-toolchains-and-compilation", "label": "Two flag pairs, header compilation, strict deps, Error Prone under Bazel — REVISE after wave 2 and owner Q6", "brief": "Write the Java-toolchain half of the depth file the sibling bazel-quality set is\nmissing, routing into its twelve existing files rather than restating them.\n\nRead first: .agents/research/bazel-topic-map.md's \"Artifact set decision\"\n(read-only, on main) so the file matches the sibling's shape exactly — no\nmanifest glob of its own, task-worded routing, one BZL- family, and no restating\nof bzlmod, hermeticity, caching, testing, CI, flags or architecture mechanics.\n\nFetch, primary only: bazel.build/docs/bazel-and-java; bazelbuild/rules_java\nREADME and distro/relnotes.bzl; blog.bazel.build/2026/01/20/bazel-9.html;\nbazel-contrib/rules_jvm README (contrib_rules_jvm: java_test_suite,\njava_junit5_test, the checkstyle/PMD/SpotBugs wrappers).\n\nPin down: the two parallel flag pairs and their different defaults —\n--java_language_version / --java_runtime_version for application code (runtime\ndefaults to local_jdk) versus --tool_java_language_version /\n--tool_java_runtime_version for build tooling (defaulting to 11 /\nremotejdk_11); remotejdk_* versus local_jdk for hermeticity; JavaBuilder's\n--patch_module of java.compiler/jdk.compiler; header compilation via ijar/turbine\nand what it buys; strict deps (--experimental_strict_java_deps) and what it\nrejects; Error Prone under Bazel via --javacopt=\"-Xep:Name:ERROR\" and\njava_package_configuration for package-scoped overrides; and the order-dependent\ntoolchain resolution rule (\"when multiple definitions for the same operating\nsystem and CPU architecture are given, the first one is used\").\n\nDECIDE: (a) the MUST row for JDK pinning under Bazel, given grpc-java pins\nnothing at all; (b) whether a bytecode-target-below-toolchain split is a\nrecommended pattern and how it is expressed; (c) whether contrib_rules_jvm's\nlint wrappers belong in the file or are a CONSIDER.\n\nTest against, all measured: bazelbuild/bazel@948b8c70e2:.bazelrc:70-73 pins\nlanguage, runtime AND tool versions at 25; rules_java@4206909b6d:.bazelrc:4-7\ndeliberately pins Java 8 because a rules repo must keep building for old\ncallers; rules_kotlin@7c51dd1210 pins only the runtime (remotejdk_17);\nrules_jvm_external@449754dcbb pins 17 and sets --experimental_strict_java_deps=strict;\ndagger@4fbc045d2b:.bazelrc:17,22-28 pins build/tool at 17 with\n--javacopt=\"-source 8 -target 8\" for output and one Error Prone severity\noverride; grpc-java@fc4314419d:.bazelrc:1-3 carries only C++ flags."}, {"group": "bazel-java", "group_label": "Bazel: the file the sibling set does not have", "id_family": "BZL-JAVA", "slug": "bazel-java-external-deps-and-kotlin", "label": "maven_install, repinning, and the Kotlin traps that have no Gradle analogue — REVISE after wave 2", "brief": "Write the external-dependency and Kotlin half of the BZL-JAVA file.\n\nFetch, primary only: bazel-contrib/rules_jvm_external README (fetch the current\none, not only the clone at 449754dcbb); bazelbuild/rules_kotlin README and\ndocs/kotlin.md; the rules_jvm_external releases page to confirm the current\nversion and whether any merge with bazel-contrib/rules_jvm has happened — the\nrecent-shifts scout flagged this as unverified and it must be settled by a\ndirect fetch of both repos' READMEs, not by search.\n\nPin down: the three-step pinning process (add lock_file, touch\nmaven_install.json + BUILD.bazel, REPIN=1 bazel run @maven//:pin) and what\nchanges once pinned — SHA-256 caching by Bazel's downloader, cross-workspace\nsharing, fully offline builds after one bazel fetch; fail_if_repin_required =\nTrue turning a stale lock from a warning into a failure, and\nRULES_JVM_EXTERNAL_REPIN=1 for scoping; the lock file's own shape (an\n__AUTOGENERATED_FILE_DO_NOT_MODIFY_THIS_FILE_MANUALLY header and an\n__INPUT_ARTIFACTS_HASH map, with drift detected by re-hashing the declared\nartifact list rather than by a version field); version_conflict_policy pinned\nversus highest-wins; strict_visibility; excluded_artifacts; neverlink and\ntestonly; fail_on_missing_checksum; and the artifact() macro's documented\ntooling cost (it hides the real target label from buildozer).\n\nThen the two Kotlin traps with no Gradle analogue: naive java_import of a Kotlin\njar producing an ijar that \"does not know about kotlin metadata with respect to\ninlined functions, and will remove method bodies inappropriately\" — establish\nwhich rules_jvm_external versions have the fix; and kt_kotlinc_options defaulting\nx_lambdas to \"class\" while Kotlin 2.x and Gradle default to \"indy\", producing\ndifferent bytecode for the same source across build systems. Also cover\npersistent and multiplex workers being on by default for KotlinCompile,\nKotlinKsp2 and JdepsMerge, and the --strategy=<mnemonic>=local opt-out.\n\nDECIDE: (a) whether committing maven_install.json plus fail_if_repin_required is\na MUST; (b) what the rule says about x_lambdas in a repo that also builds with\nGradle; (c) whether the dual-build source-of-truth question (M-X-07) gets a rule\nor only a reading heuristic, given nothing in the corpus verifies parity and\ndagger's answer is a CONTRIBUTING line plus CI job ordering."}, {"group": "maven-and-ant", "group_label": "Maven and Ant: the other half of the ecosystem", "id_family": "MVN-BUILD", "slug": "maven4-lifecycle-and-plugins", "label": "What Maven 4 changes, once we know whether it shipped — REVISE after wave 2", "brief": "Settle the Maven build half: lifecycle, plugin configuration, and what a 3→4\nmove actually breaks.\n\nFetch, primary only: maven.apache.org/guides/introduction/introduction-to-the-lifecycle.html,\n/whatsnewinmaven4.html, /guides/mini/guide-migration-to-mvn4.html,\n/plugins/maven-compiler-plugin/, /surefire/maven-surefire-plugin/,\n/guides/mini/guide-reproducible-builds.html, /pom/asf/, /wrapper/;\nand maven.apache.org/docs/history.html plus a Central query for\norg.apache.maven:maven-core:4.0.0 to settle GA.\n\nPin down: the 23 default-lifecycle phases and why invoking a hyphenated\nintermediate phase directly (integration-test) skips reports and can leave a\ncontainer hanging, making mvn verify the entry point; the Maven 4 changes with a\nbreaking consequence — Java 17 required to run Maven itself, duplicate plugin\ndeclarations becoming a hard failure, the build-POM/consumer-POM split at model\n4.1.0 with opt-in flattening, subprojects replacing modules, the lifecycle\nbecoming a tree with before:/after: hooks and numeric ordering, Plexus DI removed\nso Maven-2-era plugins break, mvnenc replacing password obfuscation, mvnup as\nthe migration tool, deployAtEnd defaulting true, -b concurrent; the compiler\nplugin's Java-8 default for source/target regardless of the running JDK and why\nrelease is the fix; Surefire's single JUnit-Platform provider and the 3.6.0-M1\nmilestone status; and reproducible-by-default from 4.0.0-beta-5 with\nproject.build.outputTimestamp as an override.\n\nDECIDE: (a) the migration-readiness checklist a Maven 3 project runs today, in\norder; (b) whether the JVM rule set targets Maven 3, Maven 4, or both, and how a\nrow states which; (c) whether the ASF parent POM's enforced floors\n(minimalMavenBuildVersion 3.9, minimalJavaBuildVersion, requirePluginVersions,\nReproducible-Builds compliance since parent v22) are worth recommending to a\nnon-ASF project as a template."}, {"group": "maven-and-ant", "group_label": "Maven and Ant: the other half of the ecosystem", "id_family": "MVN-ANT", "slug": "ant-recognise-and-migrate", "label": "You opened a build.xml: what it encodes and how to leave — REVISE after owner Q5", "brief": "Produce the recognise-and-migrate content for the one build system this program\ncovers without ever recommending it.\n\nFetch, primary only: ant.apache.org/manual/index.html, /using.html,\n/Tasks/javac.html, /Tasks/jar.html, /Tasks/signjar.html;\ndocs.gradle.org/current/userguide/ant.html; maven.apache.org's \"Using Ant with\nMaven\" guide and the maven-antrun-plugin page.\n\nPin down: Ant's core model (a project of targets, targets of tasks, ordering by\ndependency, properties set once and interpolated) and the six things a\nmigration must preserve, each with what it maps to in Gradle and in Maven —\ntargets-as-tasks, filesets and patternsets, custom taskdefs, fork settings,\nmanifest content, and signing. Then the two facts that decide the migration\nstrategy: javac's includeantruntime defaults in an environment-sensitive way\n(governed by build.sysclasspath) and Ant's own manual says \"it is usually best to\nset this to false so the script's behavior is not sensitive to the environment\nin which it is run\"; and Gradle's ant.importBuild() — the obvious\nincremental-migration tool — **disables the configuration cache the moment an Ant\nbuild is imported**, which makes \"keep build.xml alive under Gradle\" a strategy\nthat costs the single biggest Gradle performance feature.\n\nDECIDE: (a) whether the rule ever recommends ant.importBuild, given that\ntrade-off and given the corpus has 0 real usages; (b) the migration checklist,\nordered, with a stopping point that is defensible for a project that cannot\nfinish; (c) whether signjar's preservelastmodified matters enough for a\nreproducibility row.\n\nTest against: apache/ant@8c96cd6869 is the only genuine Ant build in the corpus\n(21 build.xml files, 5179 lines, root 3051) and it uses Ivy only for its own\nrelease tooling, not for main-build dependency resolution. Every other build.xml\nin the corpus is a documentation snippet or a test fixture — gradle/gradle's 20\nfiles are all snippets teaching people how to migrate away, spring-boot's 2 are\nan antlib integration-test fixture and a smoke test proving Boot repackaging\nstill works from Ant. Do not present Ant as live practice."}, {"group": "platform-and-toolchains", "group_label": "Platform and toolchains: JDK floors, determinism, and the Kotlin toolchain", "id_family": "JAVA-PLAT", "slug": "jdk-floors-modules-and-removed-apis", "label": "Choosing a JDK, declaring a module, surviving an upgrade", "brief": "Write the platform rules: what JDK a library targets, how it declares itself to\nthe module system, and what breaks on the next upgrade.\n\nFetch, primary only: openjdk.org/projects/jdk/{21,25,26,27}/; openjdk.org/jeps/403\n(strong encapsulation), /400 (UTF-8), /421 (finalization), /431 (sequenced\ncollections), /486 (security manager), /493 (jlink without JMODs), /498 (Unsafe\nwarnings), /472 (JNI restriction), /511 (module imports), /519 and /534 (compact\nobject headers); openjdk.org/projects/jigsaw/quick-start; inside.java's\n2023-05-12 Quality Outreach heads-up on SequencedCollection.\n\nPin down: the toolchain-versus---release separation and how each is expressed in\nGradle and Maven; module-info.java versus Automatic-Module-Name and what each\ngives a consumer on the module path; the strong-encapsulation consequence — since\nJDK 17 there is no global relax flag, so every reflective reach into a JDK\ninternal needs a per-module --add-opens or --add-exports chosen deliberately,\nwhich is why ORMs, mocking and serialization libraries are the recurring\ncasualties; the removed-or-disabled list with its JDK number (Security Manager\npermanently disabled JDK 24, finalization deprecated for removal JDK 18, Unsafe\nmemory-access warning JDK 24, 32-bit x86 port removed JDK 25, Applet API removed\nJDK 26); and jdeps --jdk-internals / jdeprscan as the pre-upgrade commands.\n\nAlso settle the SequencedCollection retrofit as the worked example of \"a JDK\nfeature that adds default methods to a widely-implemented interface breaks real\ncode\": the three documented incompatibility shapes (method-name conflict,\ncovariant-override conflict on a type implementing both List and Deque, and\nsilent type-inference change) and the concrete instance — Kotlin's .first()\ncolliding with the new getFirst() on Gradle's own Kotlin DSL build at JDK 21\n(gradle/gradle#27699).\n\nDECIDE: (a) the declared floor for the OCX SDK, given Spring Boot 4 and JUnit 6\nboth hold at 17 while the current LTS is 25; (b) module-info or\nAutomatic-Module-Name, with the cost of each stated; (c) the pre-upgrade\nchecklist a project runs before moving a JDK major."}, {"group": "platform-and-toolchains", "group_label": "Platform and toolchains: JDK floors, determinism, and the Kotlin toolchain", "id_family": "JAVA-PLAT", "slug": "encoding-locale-timezone-determinism", "label": "Same source, different bytes", "brief": "Write the determinism rules — the boring class that bites, and the one place the\nwhole corpus has a measured gap.\n\nFetch, primary only: openjdk.org/jeps/400; reproducible-builds.org/docs/jvm/;\ndocs.gradle.org/current/userguide/working_with_files.html and the 9.0.0 release\nnotes; maven.apache.org/guides/mini/guide-reproducible-builds.html;\ngradle/gradle#2270 (the daemon's own file.encoding).\n\nPin down: exactly which APIs JEP 400 fixed and which charset-less overloads it\ndeliberately left alone (String(byte[]), getBytes(), FileReader,\nnew PrintStream(OutputStream)); that file.encoding is a supported property only\nas UTF-8 or COMPAT since JDK 18 and anything else is undefined; that Gradle\nnever sets file.encoding for its own daemon launch, which precedes toolchain\nselection, so org.gradle.jvmargs=-Dfile.encoding=UTF-8 is still needed on a\nnon-UTF-8 host; Properties.store() always writing a timestamp comment;\njar/zip entry timestamps and ordering as the primary non-determinism source, and\nthe Gradle-9 default change (M-D-11) that wave 2 settles; and\nproject.build.outputTimestamp on the Maven side.\n\nThen the measured gap. Across all 32 exemplars: -Dfile.encoding is set in 13,\noptions.encoding or project.build.sourceEncoding in 15, -Duser.language in 2\n(guava deliberately runs its suite under hi/IN to catch locale bugs), and\n**-Duser.timezone or a TZ pin in ZERO** — in a corpus containing kafka, guava,\njackson and Exposed, all date/time-sensitive.\n\nDECIDE: (a) whether pinning a test timezone becomes a rule, at what severity,\nand what the verification is; (b) the encoding rule's exact wording for JDK 18+\nversus below, since half the fix is the build tool's own launch and not the\ncompiler; (c) whether HashMap/HashSet iteration order and classpath scan order\nget their own rows or fold into one determinism row."}, {"group": "platform-and-toolchains", "group_label": "Platform and toolchains: JDK floors, determinism, and the Kotlin toolchain", "id_family": "KT-COMP", "slug": "kotlin-toolchain-and-codegen", "label": "Compiler flags that moved, and the annotation processor that should not be kapt", "brief": "Settle the Kotlin build-side configuration: flags, targets, code generation and\ndocs, all with version numbers.\n\nFetch, primary only: kotlinlang.org/docs/compiler-reference.html,\n/gradle-compiler-options.html, /gradle-configure-project.html,\n/gradle-compilation-and-caches.html, /ksp-quickstart.html,\n/ksp-kapt-migration.html, /ksp-overview.html (the supported-libraries list),\n/dokka-introduction.html, /dokka-migration.html, and whatsnew21 through\nwhatsnew24; github.com/google/ksp README.\n\nPin down: which -X flags stabilised and when — guard conditions, non-local\nbreak/continue and multi-dollar interpolation Stable in 2.2.0 (2025-06-23),\ncontext parameters Stable in 2.4.0 — so that a rule naming -Xwhen-guards or\n-Xcontext-parameters is telling an agent to pass a flag that no longer exists;\nthat -Xjvm-default is deprecated in favour of a stable -jvm-default flag and a\njvmDefault Gradle DSL property whose **default value flipped to ENABLE in\n2.2.0**, a behaviour change rather than a rename; the JVM attribute table\n(jvmTarget defaulting to \"1.8\", jvmTargetValidationMode defaulting to ERROR,\njavaParameters, noJdk) and how jvmToolchain interacts with jvmTarget; the\nKGP/Gradle/AGP compatibility envelope as a versioned table; the Kotlin daemon's\nfour-level JVM-argument precedence; kotlin.build.report.output sinks; KSP2 as\nthe default since KSP 2.0.0 with KSP1 incompatible past Kotlin 2.3.0 or AGP 9.0,\nand whether any commonly-used processor still lacks a KSP implementation and\nforces kapt for one module; and what Dokka v1→v2 restructures (multi-module\naggregation, output directories, visibility settings) with K2 analysis stable\nfrom 2.2.0.\n\nDECIDE: (a) whether kapt may appear in a new module at all and what the rule\nsays to a project that still needs it; (b) whether -Wextra/extraWarnings is a\nrecommendation given it is still Experimental; (c) the Dokka version floor a\nrule cites, and whether Dokka's output belongs in docs-quality's world or\noutside it (M-Y-06 says the sibling's generator table has no shape for it)."}, {"group": "java-runtime-safety", "group_label": "Java runtime safety: untrusted input and failure handling", "id_family": "JAVA-SEC", "slug": "untrusted-input-and-deserialization", "label": "Every place attacker-controlled data picks a class or a path", "brief": "Write the Java security rows as a small set of shapes with named checks, not as a\nsurvey of OWASP.\n\nFetch, primary only: cheatsheetseries.owasp.org Deserialization, XML External\nEntity Prevention, Java Security, Input Validation and Cryptographic Storage\nsheets; cmu-sei.github.io SEI CERT Oracle Java rules index (the IDS, FIO, SER\nand SEC categories); github.com/FasterXML/jackson/wiki/Jackson-Polymorphic-Deserialization-CVE-Criteria\nand advisory GHSA-j3rv-43j4-c7qm (CVE-2026-54512/54513); snyk.io/research/zip-slip-vulnerability;\nfind-sec-bugs' rule list; oracle.com/java/technologies/javase/seccodeguide.html.\n\nPin down, each as a shape a reviewer can find: native deserialization of\nuntrusted data without an ObjectInputFilter; Jackson with global default typing\nor a too-broad @JsonTypeInfo base type without a current\nPolymorphicTypeValidator — and the 2026 bypass that mattered, where the validator\nchecked only the raw container class and never the nested generic type\nparameters (fixed in 2.18.8 / 2.21.4 / 3.1.4), which is why \"a validator is\npresent\" is not the check, \"the validator is current\" is; XXE per parser factory,\nbecause hardening DocumentBuilderFactory does not harden SAXParserFactory,\nXMLInputFactory, TransformerFactory, SchemaFactory, Validator, JAXB or XPath;\nJNDI lookup on untrusted input; ZipSlip; path traversal; command construction by\nconcatenation; java.util.Random where unpredictability is required.\n\nState clearly which of these core SpotBugs covers and which need find-sec-bugs,\nbecause teams assume the former.\n\nDECIDE: (a) the MUST list, and whether it lives in the java-quality index\n(always loaded) or only in the depth file; (b) whether Oracle's Secure Coding\nGuidelines section 9 (Access Control) may be cited at all, given JEP 486 removed\nthe mechanism it describes in JDK 24 — the answer shapes how the file cites its\nown sources; (c) whether the OCX SDK, which parses JSON from a subprocess it\nlaunched, is inside or outside the untrusted-input boundary, and say why."}, {"group": "java-runtime-safety", "group_label": "Java runtime safety: untrusted input and failure handling", "id_family": "JAVA-ERR", "slug": "errors-resources-and-lifecycle", "label": "Failures that vanish, and resources that do not close", "brief": "Write the error-handling and resource rows, which is where the highest-frequency\nagent mistakes live and where several checks exist but are off by default.\n\nFetch, primary only: errorprone.info/bugpatterns pages for\nInterruptedInCatchBlock, TryFailThrowable, UnusedException, CatchingUnchecked,\nMustBeClosedChecker, StreamResourceLeak; spotbugs messages.xml entries for\nOS_OPEN_STREAM, UL_UNRELEASED_LOCK, RV_RETURN_VALUE_IGNORED, DE_MIGHT_IGNORE,\nREC_CATCH_EXCEPTION, SE_NO_SERIALVERSIONID; rules.sonarsource.com/java for\nS2142, S1181, S4507 (and the deprecated S1148 it supersedes), S2095, S2057;\nthe Effective Java item list for items 69-77; openjdk.org/jeps/421.\n\nPin down which of these fire by default and which do not — this is the dive's\nmain value. CatchingUnchecked and UnusedException are in Error Prone's\nDISABLED_CHECKS, meaning the single most characteristic pattern in\nagent-written Java (wrap risky code in try/catch, log or ignore) is silent in\nevery default configuration measured. Establish the exact enabling syntax and\nwhether promoting them produces tolerable noise on a real codebase.\n\nCover: swallowed InterruptedException without re-interrupt; catch(Throwable);\ntry-with-resources including Files.walk/list/lines; ExecutorService shutdown on\nevery path; exception chaining and the new X(e.getMessage()) shape that drops the\ncause; checked-versus-unchecked selection; System.exit() in library code;\nprintStackTrace over the logger; finalize() versus Cleaner; and\nserialVersionUID on a Serializable class.\n\nDECIDE: (a) the five rows that go in the index because they must be true in\nevery edit, and everything else that stays in the depth file; (b) whether the\nthree tools that all claim resource-leak detection (SonarJava S2095, PMD\nCloseResource, Error Prone MustBeClosedChecker) get named individually or\nwhether the rule names one gate; (c) whether the sneaky-throw question — Goetz\ncalls the workarounds irresponsible, Kabutz documents the mechanics as a\npragmatic escape hatch — gets a rule or is recorded as contested with both\npositions stated."}]

const SELECTION_ITEM = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    group: { type: 'string' },
    group_label: { type: 'string' },
    id_family: { type: 'string' },
    slug: { type: 'string' },
    label: { type: 'string' },
    brief: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['group', 'group_label', 'id_family', 'slug', 'label', 'brief'],
}

const SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    path: { type: 'string' },
    wave3: { type: 'array', items: SELECTION_ITEM, description: 'the full wave-3 selection, revised briefs verbatim; at most 14 dives' },
    added_from_surprises: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'dives added or re-scoped because wave 2 surfaced them' },
    dropped: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'staged dives dropped or merged, with the reason' },
    frame_corrections: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    convergence: { type: 'string', description: 'one paragraph: did wave 2 add MUST rules and failure modes the map did not anticipate? what does that predict for wave 3 being the last?' },
  },
  required: ['path', 'wave3', 'added_from_surprises', 'dropped', 'frame_corrections', 'convergence'],
}

phase('Harvest')
const r = await agent(`Model rationale: opus — harvesting six consolidations into commissions and rewriting research briefs is a judgment task whose errors waste a whole wave of workers.

PROJECT CONTEXT (context for you, not content to reproduce):
- You are phase 6 (Iterate) of the research-lang program for the JVM ecosystem, run from ${ROOT} (a git worktree; never cd to /home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore, read it only). Wave 2 has landed. Your job: fold what it settled into the topic map, and turn the staged wave-3 briefs from guesses into commissions grounded in wave-2 results.
- Read, in this order and in full: ${ROOT}/.claude/skills/research-lang/references/wave-plan.md (Phase 3, Phase 6, Convergence); ${RESEARCH}/jvm-frame.md (Corrections first); ${RESEARCH}/jvm-topic-map.md sections "Conflicts resolved", "Artifact set decision", "Selected for wave 2", "Staged for wave 3", "Questions for the owner"; then EVERY wave-2 consolidation jvm-<group>.md for the groups gradle-core, gradle-plugin-dev, dependencies, distribution, quality-gates, language-api (list ${RESEARCH}/jvm-*.md; read each consolidation in full, especially its Verdict, Open questions and AI-agent failure modes). Skim the dive files under each jvm-<group>/ only where a consolidation cites a surprise.
- Owner decisions: none were given; the map's defaults stand (Java-first SDK with Kotlin-friendly API, JDK floor 17 / toolchain 25, Gradle floor 8.11, Kotlin DSL examples with two Groovy rows, Maven gets its own rule with Ant as a depth file, the Bazel-Java depth file is drafted here and OFFERED to bazel-quality, 90 percent coverage floor Linux-only, one docs-quality row).
- The sibling Bazel set has SHIPPED to main since the map was written: /home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/rules/bazel-quality.md and rules/bazel-quality/{architecture,bzlmod,caching,ci,cpp,flags,hermeticity,python,rust,starlark,testing,typescript}.md. Read the index (147 lines) and the routing table, and skim rust.md as the per-language exemplar the java.md must mirror. The map's drafts cite older working names (caching-rbe.md, flags-and-versions.md, javascript-typescript.md); the wave-3 bazel-java briefs must route to the SHIPPED names and match the shipped per-language file's section shape and rule-row format.

WAVE-2 RECEIPT (structured; the files are authoritative where they differ):
${JSON.stringify(WAVE2_RECEIPT, null, 1)}

STAGED WAVE-3 BRIEFS (from the map; six are marked REVISE):
${JSON.stringify(WAVE3_STAGED, null, 1)}

DO TWO THINGS.

1. APPEND to ${RESEARCH}/jvm-topic-map.md (never rewrite earlier sections; M-IDs are stable) a section "## Wave 2 landed (${DATE})" with: (a) per group, the consolidation path, the MUST count, the conflicts it resolved and the follow-ups it named; (b) "Surprises" — every finding the dives flagged that the map did not anticipate, each with a verdict: promote to a wave-3 dive, fold into an existing wave-3 brief, defer with M-ID, or reject with a reason; (c) "Map rows affected" — M-IDs whose coverage changed from uncovered to covered, and any row whose priority the evidence moved; (d) "Frame corrections" — anything wave 2 overturned in the frame or in the map's own Conflicts resolved (say which conflict number); (e) "Convergence" — did wave 2 add MUST rules and failure modes the map did not anticipate? By the wave-plan's stop condition, what must wave 3 show for the program to converge, and what is the expected residue?

2. RETURN the wave-3 selection: at most 14 dives, each with the full brief (10-25 lines, a professional commission: what to investigate, which sources to fetch — reuse URLs the scouts and wave-2 dives already found — which flags/APIs/plugin ids/rule names/versions to pin down, what exemplar or wave-2 evidence to test against, what to DECIDE). Rewrite the six REVISE briefs against the wave-2 results they depend on (name the settled fact in the brief); keep the others unless a surprise changes them; add dives only for surprises whose consolidation said "deserves another round" AND that no wave-3 brief already covers; drop or merge a staged dive if wave 2 already answered it (say so). The two bazel-java briefs must instruct the worker to read the shipped rules/bazel-quality/rust.md and index and to produce findings in that file's shape. Keep the group slugs and families exactly as staged unless you state a reason.

Rules: no claim without a source key, a path, or a rule ID; version-tag every era-specific line; use read-only tools except for the one append to jvm-topic-map.md. Return the structured receipt with the briefs verbatim.`,
  { label: 'harvest:wave2→wave3', phase: 'Harvest', model: 'opus', effort: 'high', schema: SCHEMA })

if (!r) { log('HARVEST RETURNED NULL — read journal.jsonl'); return { harvest: null } }
log(`Wave 3 commissioned: ${r.wave3.length} dives · added ${r.added_from_surprises.length} · dropped ${r.dropped.length} · frame corrections ${r.frame_corrections.length}`)
return { harvest: r }
